DERIVATION STATEMENT

`Sources/BrowserTools/Runtime/DomTreeScript.swift` builds the JavaScript
document walker this package injects into every page it reads. That walker is
derived from browser-use's `buildDomTree` script.

What travelled: the shape of the walk and its node-classification rules. A
single recursive pass over the live document that, for each node, decides
whether it is visible (`isElementVisible`), interactive, and the topmost
element at its own coordinates (`isTopElement`, an `elementFromPoint` hit
test); per-node `WeakMap` caches for bounding rects, computed styles and
interactivity so those queries are not repeated per ancestor; and a flat node
map returned to the host in one `Runtime.evaluate` round trip rather than a
tree walked over the wire.

What is this package's own: element identity and everything downstream of it.
browser-use numbers the nodes it keeps with a per-walk highlight index;
this package derives a stable id (`alohaIdFor`) by hashing an authored
identity (`#id`, `[data-testid]`) or the node's frame-scoped xpath, writes it
back onto the element as an `aloha-id` attribute so the node can be relocated
by selector on a later call, and clears page-authored values of that attribute
before each walk. Also this package's own: the occlusion probe
(`probeOccluder`), the sensitive-field value masking injected into the walker
(`Sources/BrowserTools/Runtime/SensitiveFieldScript.swift`), the packaging of
the script as a Swift string template, and the markdown serialization of the
returned node map (`Sources/BrowserTools/Context/`).

